The last four findings of the implementation audit (notes/audit-report.md).

MF3: a type parameter reachable ONLY through a capability list was invisible to
GetAllGenericTys, which walks typeArgs — and capability lists deliberately sit
outside typeArgs so they never reach code generation. The unsubstituted
placeholder then reached capability checking, where it was both accepted as
"unchecked" on the demand side (silently dropping a real requirement) and
rejected on the supply side (refusing legal code). The traversal now also
enqueues capability entries. This does not make capability lists participate in
DECIDING type arguments (proposal 3.6 forbids that); it only completes the
substitution that instantiation is handed.

MF4: inferred lists were demanded verbatim at generic call sites, still naming
the callee's own type parameters, so a requirement could be discharged at the
wrong instantiation while the real exception escaped. They are now instantiated
through the receiver's mapping combined with the call's type arguments.

MF9: proposal 3.8 covers "a method or property accessor", but accessors are not
member signatures in their own right and never reached override checking. Parent
and child getters and setters are now paired explicitly.

MF8: a non-private static class member can be redefined by a subclass and is
dispatched virtually, so 3.8 applies — but override checking runs before
inference and cannot see an inferred list. Such members now require an explicit
clause rather than being inferred, which keeps the two consistent. The fuller
fix (a post-inference re-check that would let them be inferred too) is recorded
as follow-up in the ledger.

Gate: CheckedExceptions 55/63 (the 8 failures are the three features not yet
implemented); ctest 49/49; Parser/Sema LLT 3934/3934; clang-format clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
